  <h1>Common Defamation Misconceptions</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>&ldquo;Opinions can&rsquo;t be defamatory.&rdquo;</h2><p>Not exactly. Pure opinions that cannot be proven true or false are protected. But opinions that imply underlying false facts (&ldquo;I think he embezzled money&rdquo;) can be actionable if they imply a provably false factual basis. Courts use a totality-of-circumstances test.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;Online platforms are liable for what users post.&rdquo;</h2><p>Generally no. Section 230 of the Communications Decency Act immunizes platforms (Facebook, Twitter/X, Yelp) from liability for third-party content. Your claim is against the user who posted, not the platform hosting it.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;I just need to show the statement was hurtful.&rdquo;</h2><p>Defamation requires a false statement of fact. Hurtful truths are not defamatory. Hurtful opinions are generally protected. The statement must be objectively false and capable of being proven false.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;Public figures can easily sue for defamation.&rdquo;</h2><p>The opposite. <em>New York Times v. Sullivan</em> makes it very hard for public figures to win defamation claims. They must prove actual malice &mdash; that the defendant knew the statement was false or acted with reckless disregard for truth. Most defamation suits by public figures fail.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;A retraction eliminates my claim.&rdquo;</h2><p>Not entirely. A retraction may reduce damages (particularly punitive damages) and is required before suit in some states. But it does not eliminate liability for harm already caused. A prompt, prominent retraction does significantly reduce damages in most jurisdictions.</p>
